use std
into cloth.amni

Imports: os, binary, gfx, math, gfx.linalg, gfx.gltf, gfx.motion, gfx.scene3d and spring (the local module spring.amni; its `mquat`, `pos`, `qconj` and `clamp01` are used unqualified). This file is a module with no main. It simulates garment fabric: every garment has a cage of points that are skin joints named `cloth_<garment>_<k>`, the render mesh is skinned to those joints, and each frame this module moves the points with position-based dynamics (gravity, distance and bend constraints, a maximum distance from where the body's skin would carry the point, and collision with body capsules), then poses each joint with the point's position and the rotation that best matches its neighbours. Normalize every vector with `linalg::unit3`; rotate vectors with `linalg::qrotate`; points through matrices with `linalg::point`.

The cage is built here at load time, before the avatar is added to the scene: garments are chosen by a small text config (one garment per line: mesh name, cage spacing, pinned band below the garment's top, and the free distance at its bottom, separated by spaces, all in meters), read from a file or, when there is none, the single line "skirt 0.025 0.02 0.15".

Module state: `var npts = 0`, `var item = -1`, `var sk = -1`, `var ready = false`; per point: `var p_node int[>..]` (global glTF node), `var p_j int[>..]` (4 per point), `var p_w f32[>..]` (4 per point), `var p_md f32[>..]`, `var p_rest float3[>..]`, `var p_x float3[>..]`, `var p_prev float3[>..]`, `var p_anc float3[>..]`, `var p_q linalg::Quat[>..]`, `var p_im f32[>..]` (0 for pinned points, else 1); neighbours for shape matching as `var nb_first int[>..]` (npts + 1 offsets) and `var nbr int[>..]`; edges `var e_a int[>..]`, `var e_b int[>..]`, `var e_l f32[>..]`, `var e_k f32[>..]`; caps `var c_a int[>..]`, `var c_b int[>..]`, `var c_r0 f32[>..]`, `var c_r1 f32[>..]`; `var pal gfx::mat4[>..]`.

`setup_garment(asset int, gnode int, cell f32, pin f32, free f32)` builds one garment's cage. `nf = gltf::assets[asset].nodes.first`; the garment's vertices are those of every primitive of `gltf::meshes[gltf::nodes[gnode].mesh]` (vertex v of a primitive is global vertex `prim.vfirst + v`, its floats at `* gltf::VERTEX_FLOATS`: position 0..2, joints 18..21 as floats, weights 22..25; triangle indices are `gltf::indices[prim.ifirst ..]`, local to the primitive). Collect the global vertex list `gv`. Sample: in order, accept a vertex as a cage seed when no seed accepted so far is within `cell` of it (plain loop over the seeds). With m seeds, label every garment vertex with its nearest seed (`lab`) and set each cage point's rest position to the mean of its labelled vertices. Adjacency: a `bool` list of m * m, set for both orders of every pair of different labels along each triangle edge; structural edges are the pairs i < j with adjacency; bend edges are the pairs i < k, not adjacent, sharing a neighbour j (loop i, its neighbours j, their neighbours k, deduplicated with a second m * m list). Anchor weights: for each cage point, add up its vertices' joint weights into a list of the skin's joint count, take the four largest (index and weight) and normalize them (when they sum to 0 use the skin's joint of the node "pelvis" with weight 1). Max distance: with `top` and `bot` the garment vertices' largest and smallest y, `md = free * clamp01((top - pin - y) / max(top - pin - bot, 0.000001))`.
New joints: with `pv = gltf::find_node(asset, "pelvis")` and its inverse bind matrix `ib` (from the skin, as `ibm_of(pv)`), for each cage point k push a `gltf::Node { parent: pv, mesh: -1, skin: -1, children: gltf::Span { 0, 0 }, t: linalg::point(ib, C_k), r: mquat(ib), s: v3(1, 1, 1), matrix: gfx::mat4_identity, has_matrix: false, weights: gltf::Span { 0, 0 }, name: gltf::Span { 0, 0 } }` (fill any other field with its default). Then rebuild three spans at the end of their arrays: the pelvis's children (`gltf::ints`: its old children, then the new nodes), the skin's joints (`gltf::ints`: old joints, then the new nodes) and its inverse binds (`gltf::matrices`: old ones, then `gfx::translate(-C_k)` per new joint); set `gltf::assets[asset].nodes.count` up by m. Re-skin the garment: for each garment vertex find its four nearest cage points (keep the best four while looping over all), weights `1 / max(d, 0.0001)^2` normalized, and write joints `old joint count + cage index` (as f32) into floats 18..21 and the weights into 22..25. Append the points (node, anchor joints and weights, max distance, rest `C_k`, `p_x`/`p_prev`/`p_anc` the rest, `p_q` identity, `p_im` 0 when md < 0.0001 else 1) and the edges (structural stiffness 1, bend 0.15, rest length the distance between the rest positions; point indices offset by the points already present).

`ibm_of(node int) gfx::mat4` is the node's inverse bind matrix in skin `sk` (`gltf::matrices[inverse_binds.first + k]` for the joint k with `gltf::ints[joints.first + k] == node`), or the identity when it is not a joint.

`cap_r(a int, b int, k f32) float2` measures the body around the segment from joint a to joint b in bind space (positions `pos(linalg::inverse(ibm_of(x)))`): over the vertices of the asset's "skin" node mesh whose weights on the skin joints of a and b add up to at least 0.6, with `t` the projection onto the segment (skip outside [0, 1]) and `r` the distance from it, collect r for t < 0.35 into `lo` and t > 0.65 into `hi`; return `(min(q(lo), q(hi) * 1.1), q(hi))` with `q` the k quantile (sort ascending with insertion sort, element at `floor(len * k)` clamped; 0.07 when empty).

`cloth_setup(path const byte[:], asset int) int` runs before the avatar is added to the scene. Read the config with the unqualified `read_file(path, data)` into a `byte[>..]`; when that fails use "skirt 0.025 0.02 0.15". Set `sk` like the spring module does (the skin of the "skin" node when it has a mesh and skin, else of the first node with both; return 0 when none). For each config line with four fields, find the asset's node whose mesh name equals the first field (skip when none) and call `setup_garment` with the three numbers. When any points were made, add the caps for the joint pairs (thigh_l, calf_l), (thigh_r, calf_r), (calf_l, foot_l), (calf_r, foot_r) whose nodes exist, with radii `cap_r(a, b, 0.99)`, and one for (thigh_l, thigh_r) with both radii the second value of `cap_r(pelvis, pelvis, 0.95)` measured along the thigh_l to thigh_r segment (use that segment for t and r, and the pelvis joint's weight for the 0.6 test). Build the neighbour lists from the edges with stiffness at least 0.99 (both directions; count per point, prefix sums into nb_first, then fill). Set `npts` and return it.

`cloth_open(it int)` sets `item = it`.

`anchors()` sets `motion::joints(sk, pal)` and then, for every point i, `p_anc[i]` to the sum over its four joints of `p_w[4 i + c] * linalg::point(pal[p_j[4 i + c]], p_rest[i])`.

`collide(i int, s f32) ` pushes point i out of every cap: with `A = pos(motion::world[c_a[z]])`, `B = pos(motion::world[c_b[z]])`, `ab = B - A`, `t = clamp01(dot3(x - A, ab) / max(dot3(ab, ab), 0.000001))`, `c = A + ab * t`, `r = (c_r0[z] + (c_r1[z] - c_r0[z]) * t + 0.006) * s`, `d = x - c`, `l = length3(d)`: when `l < r`, set `x = c + (if l > 0.000001 { d / l } else { unit3(p_anc[i] - c) }) * r`. Store x back into `p_x[i]`.

`limit(i int, s f32)` keeps point i within its max distance of its anchor: `e = p_x[i] - p_anc[i]`, `l = length3(e)`, `m = p_md[i] * s`; when `l > m`, `p_x[i] = p_anc[i] + (if l > 0.000001 { e * (m / l) } else { e })`.

`rotations(its int)` finds each point's rotation by shape matching with its neighbours (the method of Müller et al. 2016): for each point i, build the three columns of `A = sum over neighbours j of (p_x[j] - p_x[i]) * transpose(p_rest[j] - p_rest[i])`, that is column c is `sum (p_x[j] - p_x[i]) * (p_rest[j] - p_rest[i]).c` for c in x, y, z; then `its` times: with `q = p_q[i]`, `r0 = qrotate(q, v3(1, 0, 0))`, `r1 = qrotate(q, v3(0, 1, 0))`, `r2 = qrotate(q, v3(0, 0, 1))`, `om = (cross3(r0, a0) + cross3(r1, a1) + cross3(r2, a2)) * (1 / (abs(dot3(r0, a0) + dot3(r1, a1) + dot3(r2, a2)) + 0.000000001))`, `w = length3(om)`; stop when w < 0.000000001; else `p_q[i] = linalg::qnorm(linalg::qmul(linalg::qaxis(om / w, w), q))`.

`cloth_step(dt f32)` returns when `npts == 0`, or when the placement scale `s = length3(s3d::items[item].place.c0.xyz)` is below 0.001 (no pose from the page yet). Call `anchors()`. When `!ready || dt <= 0`: set every `p_x` and `p_prev` to its anchor; when `!ready`, set every `p_q` to `mquat(motion::world[p_node[i]])` composed after nothing (just that), run `rotations(20)`, and set `ready = true`. Otherwise, with `h = min(dt, 1 / 30)` and 3 substeps of `hs = h / 3`: each substep, for every point: when `p_im[i] == 0`, `p_prev[i] = p_x[i]` and `p_x[i] = p_anc[i]`; else `v = (p_x[i] - p_prev[i]) * 0.985`, `p_prev[i] = p_x[i]`, `p_x[i] = p_x[i] + v + v3(0, -9.81, 0) * (hs * hs)`. Then 7 iterations of: every edge with `wa = p_im[e_a]`, `wb = p_im[e_b]`, `sw = wa + wb`, skipping when sw is 0: `d = p_x[b] - p_x[a]`, `l = length3(d)`, skipping when l < 0.000000001; `c = d * ((l - e_l[k] * s) / l * e_k[k] / sw)`; `p_x[a] = p_x[a] + c * wa`; `p_x[b] = p_x[b] - c * wb`; then every point with `p_im[i] > 0`: `collide(i, s)` and `limit(i, s)`. After the substeps run `rotations(3)`.
Finally pose the joints: for each point i with node n and parent `pn = gltf::nodes[n].parent` (skip when pn < 0): `wp = motion::world[pn]`, `iw = linalg::inverse(wp)`, `ps = length3(wp.c0.xyz)`; call `s3d::set_node(item, n, linalg::point(iw, p_x[i]), linalg::qnorm(linalg::qmul(qconj(mquat(wp)), p_q[i])), v3(s / ps, s / ps, s / ps))`.
